  <p class="secondary">Foundational paper · 1964</p>
  <h1>Inhomogeneous Electron Gas</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Hohenberg and Kohn ask whether the ground state of interacting electrons in an external potential can be formulated with the three-dimensional density rather than the full many-electron wavefunction. Their exact answer comes first; the paper then explores two controlled ways to approximate the still-unknown universal functional.
  </p>
  <p class="paper-meta">
    P. Hohenberg and W. Kohn, <cite>Physical Review</cite> 136, B864–B871 (1964). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.136.B864">DOI 10.1103/PhysRev.136.B864</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the paper begins with the inhomogeneous electron gas</h2>
  <p>
    The opening contrast is historical but also conceptual. The homogeneous electron gas had a developed many-body description in terms of particles and collective excitations. Thomas–Fermi-type approaches instead treated an inhomogeneous system through a spatial density. The second route was economical, but its status was unclear: was the density merely a crude approximation variable, or could it support an exact ground-state theory?
  </p>
  <p>
    Part I of the paper isolates that question from any particular approximation. The authors consider electrons in a large box, moving in a scalar external potential <math class="math-inline" aria-label="v of r"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math> and repelling one another through the Coulomb interaction. In atomic units their Hamiltonian is organized as
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The Hamiltonian equals kinetic energy plus external-potential energy plus electron-electron interaction energy">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;=\hat&#123;T&#125;+\hat&#123;V&#125;+\hat&#123;U&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Here <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T hat"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;T&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is electronic kinetic energy, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="V hat"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;V&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> couples the density to the chosen external potential, and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="U hat"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;U&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is the fixed electron–electron repulsion. The separation matters because the theorem varies <math class="math-inline" aria-label="v"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> while holding the kinetic operator and interaction law fixed. “Universal” will mean independent of that external potential, not independent of the particle statistics, interaction, or declared physical setting.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hohenberg and Kohn, Eqs. (1)–(5), pp. B864–B865.</p>

  <h2>The density determines the external potential</h2>
  <p>
    The first exact step is a uniqueness argument. Assume, as the paper does for simplicity, a nondegenerate ground state. Suppose two external potentials <math class="math-inline" aria-label="v and v prime"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mtext> and </mtext><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\text&#123; and &#125;v'</annotation></semantics></math>, differing by more than an additive constant, produced the same ground-state density <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n of r"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>. Their ground states must be different because they solve different Schrödinger equations. Applying the ground-state variational inequality once in each direction gives
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The two variational inequalities for different external potentials and their impossible sum">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.65em">
          <mtr><mtd><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>&lt;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>&lt;</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>∫</mo><mo>[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>&lt;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E'&lt;E+\int[v'(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)-v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)]n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;,\qquad E&lt;E'+\int[v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)-v'(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)]n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;,\qquad E+E'&lt;E+E'.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read the first two lines as two legitimate upper-bound comparisons. If both systems really had the same density, the potential-difference integrals would cancel when the inequalities were added. The final line is impossible. Therefore the starting assumption fails: within the paper’s setting, the ground-state density determines the external potential up to a constant. That potential fixes the Hamiltonian, so it also fixes the ground state and all of its ground-state properties.
  </p>
  <p>
    Formally, once the density fixes the external potential, it fixes the Hamiltonian and therefore its spectrum within the same particle-number and interaction setting. But the theorem does not provide an excitation-energy functional or a practical route from the density to that spectrum, and it does not turn the ground-state density into an excited-state observable. Nor is every nonnegative function integrating to an integer automatically the ground-state density of some external potential. The paper itself flags this representability question in a footnote; later formulations make the density domain more precise.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hohenberg and Kohn, Eqs. (6)–(8) and footnote 12, p. B865.</p>

  <figure class="paper-argument" aria-labelledby="hk-proof-caption">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Assume</strong><span>two genuinely different potentials give the same density</span></li>
      <li><strong>Compare</strong><span>each ground state in the other Hamiltonian</span></li>
      <li><strong>Add</strong><span>the two strict variational inequalities</span></li>
      <li><strong>Reject</strong><span>the impossible inequality and recover uniqueness</span></li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption id="hk-proof-caption">The theorem’s central proof is short because the density makes the external-potential terms cancel. The physical assumptions that make the inequalities strict still matter.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The second step is a density variational principle</h2>
  <p>
    Uniqueness makes it possible to regard the ground-state wavefunction as a functional of its density. Hohenberg and Kohn therefore collect the parts of the energy that do not depend explicitly on the external potential into one functional, then restore the potential-dependent term separately:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The universal functional and total energy functional of the density">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.65em">
          <mtr><mtd><mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⟨</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>|</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>⟩</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>∫</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mo>∫</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>N</mi><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[n]=\langle\Psi[n]|\hat&#123;T&#125;+\hat&#123;U&#125;|\Psi[n]\rangle,\qquad E_v[n]=\int v(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+F[n],\qquad \int n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;=N.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The notation makes the division of responsibility visible. <math class="math-inline" aria-label="F of n"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[n]</annotation></semantics></math> contains kinetic energy and electron–electron interaction for the fixed interaction law; it is the same functional for every allowed external potential. The integral is the only explicit place where the particular nuclei or other external field enter. Minimizing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E sub v of n"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_v[n]</annotation></semantics></math> over the paper’s admissible densities selects the correct ground-state density and energy.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the exact foundation, but it is not yet a recipe for calculation. The proof establishes that <math class="math-inline" aria-label="F of n"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[n]</annotation></semantics></math> exists. It does not reveal a simple exact expression for it. The paper says plainly that the main many-electron difficulty has moved into determining this universal functional.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hohenberg and Kohn, Eqs. (9)–(13), p. B865.</p>

  <h2>The paper next separates the long-range Coulomb term</h2>
  <p>
    Because the classical part of the Coulomb energy is known explicitly and long-ranged, the authors remove it from the unknown remainder:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The universal functional equals the classical Coulomb density energy plus a remaining universal functional G">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>∬</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[n]=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;\iint\frac&#123;n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;')&#125;&#123;|\mathbf&#123;r&#125;-\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'|&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^3\mathbf&#123;r&#125;'+G[n].</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first term treats the density as a continuous classical charge distribution. The new functional <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G of n"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[n]</annotation></semantics></math> must still contain the electronic kinetic energy and every part of exchange and correlation not represented by that classical term. This decomposition is an ancestor of the later Kohn–Sham energy split, but it is not yet the Kohn–Sham construction: no auxiliary orbitals have appeared.
  </p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hohenberg and Kohn, Eqs. (14)–(18), pp. B865–B866.</p>

  <h2>Two density regimes test what an approximation must capture</h2>
  <p>
    After the exact Part I, the paper changes task. It no longer proves a universal theorem; it investigates how <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G of n"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[n]</annotation></semantics></math> can be described in two limiting regimes using information from a uniform electron gas. Keeping that change of status visible prevents a common reading error: the later approximations are not additional statements of the Hohenberg–Kohn theorem.
  </p>

  <div class="paper-comparison">
    <section>
      <h3>Part II · Almost constant density</h3>
      <p>The density is a small-amplitude departure from a constant background. Its spatial variation may still contain short wavelengths.</p>
      <p>The functional is expanded in powers of the density change. The quadratic and higher kernels are connected to linear and nonlinear response functions of the uniform electron gas.</p>
      <p>This route can retain nonlocal response such as long-range Friedel oscillations; a purely local gradient expansion cannot generally do so.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>Part III · Slowly varying density</h3>
      <p>The density may change by a large amount, but only over a length scale that becomes long compared with microscopic electronic scales.</p>
      <p>The functional is expanded in spatial gradients. The leading term recovers Thomas–Fermi-type local physics, with higher derivatives correcting spatial variation.</p>
      <p>The authors stress the limitation: a gradient expansion is not built to reproduce short-wavelength shell structure or Friedel oscillations.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <p>
    These limits differ in exactly the way a quick summary can hide. “Nearly uniform” limits the amplitude of the density change but not necessarily its wavelength. “Slowly varying” limits the wavelength but allows substantial change in density. The paper’s partial resummation tries to connect advantages of the two descriptions, yet its usefulness for real atoms, molecules, and solids was still untested in the paper.
  </p>
  <div class="callout" role="note" aria-labelledby="hk-source-figures">
    <p id="hk-source-figures"><strong>Read the source figures.</strong> In Fig. 1 on p. B867, read the horizontal axis as the perturbation wavevector scaled by the Fermi wavevector, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q divided by q sub F"><semantics><mfrac><mi>q</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q/q_F</annotation></semantics></math>, and the vertical curve as the uniform electron gas polarizability. The figure labels this scale <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q sub F"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_F</annotation></semantics></math>; the neighboring formulas use the more familiar <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k sub F"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_F</annotation></semantics></math> for the same Fermi-wavevector scale. Fig. 2 on p. B868 carries that response into the kernel <math class="math-inline" aria-label="K of q"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Look near <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q equals two k sub F"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2k_F</annotation></semantics></math>: the nonanalytic change there cannot be reproduced by a power series around <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q equals zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is why a local gradient expansion misses the associated long-range Friedel oscillation. These are response and kernel diagrams for the paper’s uniform-gas analysis, not measured density profiles or evidence that the approximation is accurate for every real material.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Hohenberg and Kohn, Parts II–III and concluding remarks, pp. B866–B871.</p>

  <h2>What the paper established—and what it did not</h2>
  <dl class="source-map">
    <dt>Established in the original paper</dt>
    <dd>For the stated nondegenerate ground-state setting and fixed interaction, the ground-state density fixes the external potential up to a constant, and the exact ground-state energy obeys a density variational principle over the paper’s admissible density class.</dd>
    <dt>Constructed in the original paper</dt>
    <dd>Controlled functional descriptions for almost-constant and slowly-varying densities, expressed through uniform-electron-gas energies and response functions.</dd>
    <dt>Not supplied by the theorem</dt>
    <dd>A closed exact formula for the universal functional, a practical self-consistent orbital algorithm, or an excitation functional that recovers spectra from the ground-state density. The density–potential uniqueness result formally fixes the Hamiltonian and its spectrum, but it is not itself a practical excited-state theory.</dd>
    <dt>Not resolved by the original formulation</dt>
    <dd>A general admissible-density construction independent of external-potential representability, a full treatment of degeneracy, or the later convex analytic domain used in modern rigorous DFT.</dd>
  </dl>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Later development.</strong> Levy’s constrained search defines the universal functional by minimizing over antisymmetric wavefunctions that yield a chosen density. Lieb’s convex formulation sharpens the domain and lower-semicontinuity structure. These are essential modern clarifications, but they are not the proof organization used in the 1964 paper.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How this paper fits the Atlas</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={href('/core/part-iv/')}>Core Part IV · Density Functional Theory</a> combines the original result with the later Levy–Lieb domain and the Kohn–Sham bridge. <a href={href('/theory/density-functional-theory-foundations/')}>Foundations · Density-Functional Theory Foundations</a> provides prerequisite repair for functional and representability language. Continue with <a href={href('/reading/literature/kohn-sham-1965/')}>Kohn and Sham’s 1965 paper</a> to see how one-electron equations enter, then read <a href={href('/reading/literature/levy-1979/')}>Levy’s constrained-search paper</a> for the later density-domain repair.
  </p>

